<chapter xml:id="ch-more-speed">
  <title>More about Calculation Times</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <xref ref="ch-fast-code"/> is one of the places in this text with discussion about the speed of code and choices
      to make in algorithms.
      This chapter dives deeper into calculations involving integers, floats including the big varieties.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-speed-int-float">
    <title>Speed of Ints, Floats, BigInts and BigFloats</title>

    <p>
      In <xref ref="sec-intro-big-float"/>, we introduced the BigFloat type and noted that it is slower than the
      built-in Float64 type.
      We will see this directly as well as find some ways around this limitation.
      Simple addition and multiplication of integers or floating point numbers is extremely fast with modern computer
      chips.
      Though, there are differences between data types and operations on speed.
      Let's take a look.
    </p>

    <p>
      Consider the following code which basically adds and subtracts some random integers.
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
        @btime let 
          local s = 0
          for i=1:100_000
            s += rand(1:10) 
            s -= rand(1:10) 
          end
          s
        end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      and this returned <c>1940</c> in <c>290.375 μs</c> and recall that since there are random numbers in this, you
      will most likely get a different result, but the timing should be comparable.
    </p>

    <note>
      <p>
        Recall from <xref ref="ch-fast-code"/>, we noticed that the julia compiler can simplify calculations if, for
        example, we just added the same number 100,000 times.
        It can use formula to return the answer without going through the loop.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>
      If we adapt the code above to using Float64, with the following:
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
        @btime let 
          local s = 0.0
          for i=1:100_000
            s += rand(1.0:10.0) 
            s -= rand(1.0:10.0) 
          end
          s
        end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      This results in <c>-1651.0</c> in <c>445.208 μs</c>.
      Notice that this is about twice as slow as same loop with integers.
      This means that if floating points are used, then you should expect slower code.
    </p>

    <p>
      Next, let's look at BigInts and see how they are affected.
      If we update this loop above with the following:
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
        @btime let 
          local s = big(0)
          for i=1:100_000
            s += big(rand(1:10))
            s -= big(rand(1:10))
          end
          s
end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      the result was <c>-2134</c> and took <c>8.382 ms</c>.
      Recall that since a ms is 1000 times longer than a μs, this code runs about 25 times slower.
      Because there is some extra code (mainly the <c>big</c> command), this may not mean that multiplying BigInts is 25
      times slower than an Int64.
    </p>

    <p>
      If we increase the size of the BigInts, we also see a slowdown:
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
          @btime let 
            local s = big(0)
            for i=1:100_000
              s += rand(big(1):big(10)^100)
              s -= rand(big(1):big(10)^100)
            end
            s
          end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      and this returns a very large number in about <c>48.712 ms</c> another factor of 6 slower.
      This is basically because BigInts can grow as needed and because of this, there aren't the efficiencies that we
      see in a fixed precision integer like <c>Int64</c>.
      Additionally, there is built-in code to handle 64-bit integers and there isn't for things like BigInt.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-calculating-e">
    <title>Calculating <m>e</m></title>

    <p>
      As an example, let's look at finding a decimal approximation of <m>e</m>.
      For this, we will use the Taylor Series
      <md xml:id="eq-taylor-series-ex" number="yes">
        e^x = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{x^n}{n!} = 1 + x + \frac{x^2}{2} + \frac{x^3}{3!} + \cdots
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      If we evaluate the series in <xref ref="eq-taylor-series-ex"/> at <m>x=1</m>, then we can find an approximation to
      <m>e</m> or
      <md>
        e = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n!}
      </md>
      Also, note that due to the factorial in the denominator, this series converges quickly and hopefully we don't need
      many terms to find a good estimate.
      The following function will perform this calculation for <c>n</c> terms.
    </p>

    <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
      <code>
        function calc_e(n::Integer)
          sum = 1.0
          for k=1:n
            sum += 1/factorial(k)
          end
          sum
        end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      and we can run it like:
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
          calc_e(10)
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      which returns <c>2.7182818011463845</c>.
      We'd like a good approximation and can use Julia's <c>exp</c> function to find the exact value:
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
          abs(calc_e(10)-exp(1))
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      which returns <c>2.7312660577649694e-8</c> so we get about 7 digits of accuracy with 10 terms.
      Since the rest of this chapter will be interested in the the accuracy of calculating e, we will use the function
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
        function num_digits_e(approx::T) where T &lt;: Real
          local e_val = exp(one(T))
          approx == e_val ? floor(Int, log10(2)*precision(approx)) : floor(Int, -log10(abs(approx - e_val)))
        end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      to return the number of digits of accuracy.
      <fn>A keen eye notices that there is a equality statement with two floating points.  As noted earlier, this is not generally good practice because for that to return <c>true</c>, the two numbers must be exactly equal bitwise.  However, this can happen as we increase the accuracy of calculations. </fn>
      When called like <c>num_digits_e(calc_e(10))</c>, the result is 7.
    </p>

    <p>
      If we go to 20 terms, then we get 15 digits of accuracy and notice that any other number of terms doesn't change
      this accuracy.
      Why?
    </p>

    <p>
      I'm sure you said that all of the operations used default floating point or <c>Float64</c>, which maxes out about
      15 decimal digits.
      As we discussed in <xref ref="sec-intro-big-float"/>, we can use <c>BigFloat</c> to extend to more digits of
      accuracy.
    </p>

    <p>
      To do this in the calculating e function above, if we change the initialization of <c>sum</c> to a big as in
    </p>

    <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
      <code>
          function calc_e(n::Integer)
            sum = big(1.0)
            for k=1:n
              sum += 1/factorial(k)
            end
            sum
          end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      this would seemingly fix that issue and running <c>num_digits_e(calc_e(20))</c> returns 15, but increasing the
      number of terms to 30 results in the following error:
    </p>

    <p>
      <cd>
        <cline>OverflowError: 21 is too large to look up in the table; </cline>
        <cline>   consider using `factorial(big(21))` instead</cline>
      </cd>
    </p>

    <p>
      and the error gives the fix for this in that we wrap the <c>k</c> with a <c>big</c> or
    </p>

    <listing xml:id="list-calc-e">
      <title>Code for calculating <m>e</m></title>

      <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
        <code>
        function calc_e(n::Integer)
          sum = big(1.0)
          for k=1:n
            sum += 1/factorial(big(k))
          end
          sum
        end
        </code>
      </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
      and if we rerun <c>num_digits_e(calc_e(30))</c>, we still only get 15 digits.
      This should be a red flag that somewhere there are only regular Float64 in a calculation instead of a BigFloat.
      Looking at the length of the number that is returned from <c>calc_e</c> it appears that this function is correct.
      However, notice that in <c>num_digits</c>, we use <c>exp(1)</c>.
      Because we didn't specify that this is a BigFloat, then it is just a Float64.
      We can fix this with:
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
          num_digits_e(approx::Real) = floor(Int, -log10(abs(approx - exp(big(1.0)))))
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      and now running with <c>num_digits_e(calc_e(30))</c> gives 33, indicating that 30 terms of the taylor series
      results in 33 digits of accuracy.
      We can get a good idea the convergence of this series to <m>e</m> with the following table:
    </p>

    <table>
      <title>Convergence of Taylor Series to <m>e</m></title>

      <tabular>
        <row>
          <cell>10</cell>
          <cell>7</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>20</cell>
          <cell>19</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>30</cell>
          <cell>33</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>40</cell>
          <cell>49</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>50</cell>
          <cell>66</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>60</cell>
          <cell>75</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      and from this table, each 10 terms seems to return an additional 12 to 17 digits of accuracy.
      This isn't quite linear, but close.
      However, if you look at further terms there isn't more than 75 digits of accuracy.
      Why does this occur?
    </p>

    <p>
      In short, this is because BigFloats have some fixed accuracy to them.
      We'll look at this in the next section
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-big-float-detail">
    <title>Details of BigFloats</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        There is the built-in function, <c>precision</c> that will give the precision of a big float.
        If we just do
      </p>

      <program language="julia">
        <code>
          precision(big(1.0))
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        this returns 256, which is the number of binary digits that each BigFloat stores.
        This is 4 times larger than the default Float64 number, but notice that it appears that we get about 75 digits
        of accuracy.
        In fact, one can convert from binary to decimal digits, using
        <md xml:id="eq-binary-to-decimal" number="yes">
          d = b \log_{10}(2) + 1
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Thus the default 256 binary decimals is <m>258\log_{10}(2)+1 \approx 78</m>, which is close to what we see.
        Note: the number of stored digits doesn't always exactly match the accuracy.
      </p>

      <p>
        The inverse of this function is
        <md xml:id="eq-decimal-to-binary" number="yes">
          b = \frac{d-1}{\log_{10}(2)}
        </md>
        which can be helpful if we know that we want some number of digits of accuracy.
        For example, let's say we would like 200 digits of accuracy, then
        <md>
          b = \frac{200-1}{\log_{10}(2)} \approx 661.
        </md>
        We could set the accuracy of the BigFloat to this, however, it is more efficient to use a power of two, so the
        next higher power of 2, is <m>1024 = 2^{10}</m>.
        Using <xref ref="eq-binary-to-decimal"/>,
        <md>
          d = 1024 \log_{10}(2)+1 \approx 308
        </md>
        so this would have plenty of room.
        If we repeat this for 1000 digits, we would need <m>2^{12}</m> digits.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-big-float-precision">
      <title>Setting the Precision of a BigFloat</title>

      <p>
        There are a couple of ways to set the precision of a BigFloat.
        You can use the keyword argument <c>precision</c> when creating the BigFloat using that constructor, such as
      </p>

      <program language="julia">
        <code>
          x = BigFloat(1.0, precision = 1024)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        and this is sufficient if you just have one or two big floats on which to operate.
        However, more generally, we can set it globally with the <c>setprecision</c> function as in
      </p>

      <program language="julia">
        <code>
          setprecision(1024)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        which might be the preferred way to do this, however, often we want some middle ground, which we can do setting
        it in the following block:
      </p>

      <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
        <code>
          setprecision(1024) do 
            e1 = calc_e(100)
            num_digits_e(e1)
          end
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        and this returns <c>159</c>.
        Only within the <c>do-end</c> is the precision 1024.
      </p>

      <exercise>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Change the precision to <c>2^12</c> and determine experimentally (meaning just try some values until you
              find the result) the number of terms to the nearest 100 with at least 1000 terms of accuracy.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              How much time does it take to find this result.
              Using the <c>@btime</c> macro from <c>BenchmarkTools</c>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section>
    <title>Examining and Improving the Time</title>

    <p>
      You should have gotten 500 terms needed in order to get at least 1000 digits of accuracy.
      Using this code:
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
          @btime setprecision(2^12) do 
            e1 = calc_e(500)
            num_digits_e(e1)
          end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      My computer showed that it took <c>2.172 ms</c> to get this.
      We would like to improve the speed, but let's be careful.
      Note that the timing is on three separate tasks 1) setting the precision 2) calculating <m>e</m> and 3)
      determining the number of digits.
      If we really just interested in the speed of calculating <m>e</m>, let's time that only.
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
          setprecision(2^12) do 
            @btime e1 = calc_e(500)
          end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      And the result was <c>1.764 ms</c>, implying that calculating the number of digits is about <c>0.4 ms</c>.
      <fn>If you just time the <c>num_digits</c> line, this would give you a sense of that.</fn>
    </p>

    <p>
      Let's see if we can improve this.
      Recall that this calculation is just a sum of a number of terms of the form <m>1/k!</m> and there is a division
      and a factorial.
    </p>

    <p>
      If we sum through 500 terms, the largest value of this is <m>500!</m>, which takes a bit of time with
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
          @btime factorial(big(500))
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      which results in 1.288 μs on my computer.
      This isn't too long, but not trivial either, remembering that you have 500 calculations.
      Also, since there is also the reciprocal of this, timing the following:
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
          @btime 1/factorial(big(500))
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      results in <c>1.462 μs</c>, and it's same to assume that the division was about 200 μs.
      As we saw above, division with BigFloats is expensive.
    </p>

    <p>
      We will show a method to improve the calculations of the factorial and will do something similar for the division
      later in this chapter.
    </p>

    <p>
      Notice that in the for loop of <xref ref="list-calc-e"/>, each term calculates the full factorial in that if we
      known the previous factorial, we can use this to calculate the current one.
      <fn>This is how we calculated the factorial recursively  in <xref ref="sect-recursion"/>, however, here we are not doing it recursively, which we have noted is slow.</fn>
      Basically, we can use the property that
      <md>
        n! = n (n-1)!
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      We can update the function in <xref ref="list-calc-e"/> to
    </p>

    <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
      <code>
        function calc_e2(n::Integer)
          sum = big(1.0)
          fact = big(1)
          for k=1:n
            fact *= k
            sum += 1/fact
          end
          sum
        end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      and then testing this with
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
        setprecision(2^12) do 
          e1 = @btime calc_e2(500)
          num_digits_e(e1)
        end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      the results on my computer is <c>1.452 ms</c> and I got about 18% faster than that in <xref ref="list-calc-e"/>.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is better, but one other thing to notice is that in each step in the loop of the function <c>calc_e2</c>,
      there is a multiplication (line 5), a division (line 6) and an addition (line 6).
      We can cut this down to two operations with
    </p>

    <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
      <code>
          function calc_e3(n::Integer)
            sum = big(1.0)
            term = big(1.0)
            for k=1:n
              term /= big(k)
              sum += term
            end
            sum
          end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      and timing it with
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
        setprecision(2^12) do 
          e1 = @btime calc_e3(500)
          num_digits_e(e1)
        end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      results in 305.333 μs, quite a speed up and in fact more than 80% faster than the original.
    </p>

    <p>
      This seems like the best that we can do, however, one thing that makes this <q>slow</q> is that there is floating
      point division throughout the algorithm.
      The next section will eliminate this.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-binary-splitting">
    <title>Binary Splitting Algorithm</title>

    <p>
      Consider a series of the form
      <md>
        s = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} S_k
      </md>
      where <m>S_k</m> is a rational number of the form
      <md>
        S_k = S_{k-1} \cdot \frac{P_k L_k}{Q_k}
      </md>
      where <m>P_k, Q_k</m> and <m>L_k</m> are integers for all <m>k</m>.
      Although it seems that we could combine <m>P_k</m> and <m>L_k</m>, in general, <m>L_k</m> can be a linear term of
      the form <m>\ell_0 + \ell_1 k</m>,
      <fn>In <xref ref="ch-calc-pi"/>, we will revisit this method with a linear term.</fn> but will consider this to be
      1 simplicity in the rest of this chapter.
    </p>

    <p>
      We also define
      <md>
        T_k = P_k L_k.
      </md>
      This is the numerator of the series but as we take partial sums, it will represent the overall numerator of the
      partial sum with the product of the <m>Q_i</m> terms as the overall denominator.
    </p>

    <p>
      We're going to show that we can take a partial sum of this, split it and combine it efficiently.
      So define
      <md>
        s_{a..b} = \sum_{k=a}^{b-1} \frac{P(k)}{Q(k)}.
      </md>
      to be the partial sum on <m>[a,b)</m>, so not including <m>b</m>.
      The power of this method comes from splitting <m>s_p</m> into a left and right parts.
      If <m>a \lt m \lt b</m>, then
      <md>
        s_{a..b} = s_{a..m} + s_{m..b}
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Consider a four-term partial sum:
      <md>
        <mrow>s_{0..4} \amp = S_0 + S_1 + S_2 + S_3 </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = \frac{P_0}{Q_0} + \frac{P_1 P_0}{Q_1 Q_0} + \frac{P_2 P_1 P_0}{Q_2 Q_1 Q_0} 
            + \frac{P_3 P_2 P_1 P_0}{Q_3 Q_2 Q_1 Q_0} </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = \frac{P_0}{Q_0} + \frac{P_1 P_0}{Q_1 Q_0} + \frac{P_1 P_0}{Q_1 Q_0} \left(\frac{P_2}{Q_2} + \frac{P_2 P_3}{Q_2 Q_3}\right)</mrow>
        <mrow xml:id="eq-splitting-1" number="yes">  \amp = \frac{Q_1 P_0 + P_1 P_0}{Q_1 Q_0} + \frac{P_1 P_0}{Q_1 Q_0} \left(\frac{P_2 Q_3 + P_2 P_3}{Q_3 Q_2} \right)</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      This was written as two terms to help understand the splitting.
      Define
      <md>
        <mrow> P_L \amp = P_0 P_1 \amp \amp \text{or in general the product of numerators on the left} </mrow>
        <mrow> Q_L \amp = Q_0 Q_1 \amp \amp \text{or in general the product of denominators on the left} </mrow>
        <mrow> P_R \amp = P_2 P_3 \amp \amp \text{or in general the product of numerators on the right} </mrow>
        <mrow> Q_R \amp = Q_2 Q_3 \amp \amp \text{or in general the product of denominators on the right} </mrow>
        <mrow> T_L  \amp = Q_1P_0 + P_1 P_0 \amp \amp \text{or in general the numerator on the left} </mrow>
        <mrow> T_R  \amp = Q_3P_2 + P_2 P_3 \amp \amp \text{or in general the numerator on the right} </mrow>
      </md>
      so the above sum can be written as
      <md>
        s_{0..4} = \frac{T_L}{Q_L} + \frac{P_L}{Q_L} \cdot \frac{T_R}{Q_R}
      </md>
      but in fact, it doesn't matter the number of terms this is the key to splitting.
      Also notice that this formula works for both the first term and the term in parentheses of
      <xref ref="eq-splitting-1"/>, so in generally this is performed recursively.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let's look at an example of this.
      Recall that the Taylor Series of <m>\tan^{-1} x</m> can be written
      <md>
        \tan^{-1} x = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} (-1)^k \frac{x^{2k+1}}{2k+1}
      </md>
      and for simplification, we will examine this for <m>x=1</m> so
      <md>
        s = \tan^{-1} (1) = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} (-1)^k \frac{1}{2k+1}
      </md>
      and the first few terms are
      <md>
        s = S_0 + S_1 + S_2 + S_3 + \cdots = \frac{1}{1} - \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{5} - \frac{1}{7} + \cdots
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      First, note that we required the series to have the form:
      <md>
        S_k = S_{k-1} \cdot \frac{P_k}{Q_k}
      </md>
      and the arctangent series doesn't look like this form.
      However, note that
      <md>
        <mrow>S_0 \amp = 1 </mrow>
        <mrow> S_1\amp = -\frac{1}{3} = -\frac{1}{3} S_0 </mrow>
        <mrow> S_2 \amp = \frac{1}{5} = -\frac{3}{5} S_1</mrow>
        <mrow> S_3 \amp = -\frac{1}{7} = -\frac{5}{7} S_2 </mrow>
      </md>
      and we can show in general that this series has the property that
      <md xml:id="eq-binary-relation" number="yes">
        S_k = -\frac{2k-1}{2k+1} S_{k-1}
      </md>
      and we will take <m>Q_k</m> to be positive, so let it be <m>2k+1</m> with <m>P_k = -(2k-1)</m>.
    </p>

    <image width="60%">
      <shortdescription/>
      <latex-image>
        <![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[
    >=Stealth,
    node distance = 1.2cm and 0.8cm,
    % Styles for tree nodes
    leaf/.style = {rectangle, rounded corners, draw=blue!80, fill=blue!5, thick, inner sep=6pt, align=center},
    branch/.style = {rectangle, rounded corners, draw=teal!80, fill=teal!5, thick, inner sep=6pt, align=center},
    root/.style = {rectangle, rounded corners, draw=purple!80, fill=purple!5, thick, inner sep=8pt, align=center},
    % Arrow style
    edge/.style = {draw=gray!80, thick, <-}
]

    % --- LEVEL 2: Root Node ---
    \node[root] (root) {
        \textbf{Root Block $[0, 4)$}\\[2pt]
        $P_{0..4} = P_L \cdot P_R$\\[1pt]
        $Q_{0..4} = Q_L \cdot Q_R$\\[1pt]
        $\mathbf{T_{0..4} = T_L \cdot Q_R + P_L \cdot T_R}$\\[3pt]
        \textit{Final Sum:} $\frac{T_{0..4}}{Q_{0..4}}$
    };

    % --- LEVEL 1: Branch Nodes ---
    \node[branch, below left=1.2cm and 1.5cm of root] (bL) {
        \textbf{Left Block $[0, 2)$}\\[2pt]
        $P_{0..2} = P_0 P_1$\\[1pt]
        $Q_{0..2} = Q_0 Q_1$\\[1pt]
        $T_{0..2} = T_0 Q_1 + P_0 T_1$
    };

    \node[branch, below right=1.2cm and 1.5cm of root] (bR) {
        \textbf{Right Block $[2, 4)$}\\[2pt]
        $P_{2..4} = P_2 P_3$\\[1pt]
        $Q_{2..4} = Q_2 Q_3$\\[1pt]
        $\mathbf{T_{2..4} = T_2 Q_3 + P_2 T_3}$
    };

    % --- LEVEL 0: Leaf Nodes ---
    \node[leaf, below left=1.2cm and 0.3cm of bL] (k0) {
        \textbf{Leaf $k=0$}\\[2pt]
        $P_0, Q_0, T_0$\\[1pt]
        $(T_0 = P_0 L_0)$
    };

    \node[leaf, below right=1.2cm and 0.3cm of bL] (k1) {
        \textbf{Leaf $k=1$}\\[2pt]
        $P_1, Q_1, T_1$\\[1pt]
        $(T_1 = P_1 L_1)$
    };

    \node[leaf, below left=1.2cm and 0.3cm of bR] (k2) {
        \textbf{Leaf $k=2$}\\[2pt]
        $P_2, Q_2, T_2$\\[1pt]
        $(T_2 = P_2 L_2)$
    };

    \node[leaf, below right=1.2cm and 0.3cm of bR] (k3) {
        \textbf{Leaf $k=3$}\\[2pt]
        $P_3, Q_3, T_3$\\[1pt]
        $(T_3 = P_3 L_3)$
    };

    % --- CONNECTIONS ---
    % Upward flow toward root
    \draw[edge] (root) -- (bL);
    \draw[edge] (root) -- (bR);
    
    \draw[edge] (bL) -- (k0);
    \draw[edge] (bL) -- (k1);
    
    \draw[edge] (bR) -- (k2);
    \draw[edge] (bR) -- (k3);

\end{tikzpicture}
]]>
      </latex-image>
    </image>

    <p>
      We will find the values of each of the leaves.
      We use <m>P_0=Q_0=1</m> for the first term and <xref ref="eq-binary-relation"/> for the rest, so
      <md>
        <mrow>P_0 \amp = 1 \amp Q_0 \amp = 1 \amp T_0 \amp =  1</mrow>
        <mrow>P_1 \amp = -1 \amp Q_1 \amp = 3 \amp  T_1 \amp =  -1</mrow>
        <mrow>P_2 \amp = -3 \amp Q_2 \amp = 5 \amp T_2 \amp =  -3</mrow>
        <mrow xml:id="eq-splitting-example-leaves" number="yes">P_3 \amp = -5 \amp Q_3 \amp = 7 \amp T_3 \amp = -5</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The next level up is found with the equations in ???
      <md>
        <mrow>P_{0..2} \amp = P_0 P_1 = -1 \amp Q_{0..2} \amp = Q_0 Q_1 =  3 \amp
        T_{0..2} =  \amp T_0 Q_1 + P_0 T_1  = 2 </mrow>
        <mrow xml:id="ex-splitting-ex-level-2" number="yes">P_{2..4} \amp = P_2 P_3 = 5 \amp Q_{2..4} \amp = Q_2 Q_3 = 35 \amp
        T_{2..4} =  \amp T_2 Q_3 + P_2 T_3  = -6 </mrow>
      </md>
      and the last operation is
      <md>
        <mrow>P_{0..4} \amp = P_{0..2} P_{2..4} = -15  \amp
          Q_{0..4} = \amp Q_{0..2} Q_{2..4} = 105 </mrow>
        <mrow> T_{0..4} \amp = T_{0..2} Q_{2..4} + P_{0..2} T_{2..4} = 2(35)  + (- 1)(-6) = 76
        </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      And the result is
      <md>
        v = \frac{T_{0..4}}{Q_{0..4}} = \frac{76}{105}
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Recall that this is the first four terms of the series and
      <md>
        1-\frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{5} - \frac{1}{7} = \frac{76}{105}
      </md>
      the same result.
      Also note that the intermediate steps can be written as
      <md>
        <mrow>\frac{T_{0..2}}{Q_{0..2}} \amp = \frac{2}{3} \amp
          \frac{P_{0..2}}{Q_{0..2}} \frac{T_{2..4}}{Q_{2..4}} \amp = \frac{-1}{3} \frac{-6}{35} = \frac{2}{35}
        </mrow>
      </md>
      and the first result is the first two terms or <m>1-1/3</m> and the second result the sum of the 3rd and 4th terms
      of the series or <m>1/5-1/7</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      When we checked the partial sum of the 3rd and 4th terms, we need to scale the fraction <m>T_{2..4}/Q_{2..4}</m>
      by the fraction <m>P_{0..2}/Q_{0..2}</m> because ????
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-bin-split-exp">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use binary splitting to find the first four terms of the series for <m>e^x</m> when <m>x=1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          In this case, note that the series:
          <md>
            e = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{k!}
          </md>
          and since we need to have a series of the form:
          <md>
            S_k = S_{k-1} \cdot \frac{P_k}{Q_k}
          </md>
          this holds with <m>P_k=1</m> for all <m>k</m> and <m>Q_k = k</m> when <m>k \geq 1</m> and <m>Q_0=1</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          The first four terms of the leaves then are
          <md>
            <mrow>P_0 \amp = 1 \amp Q_0 \amp = 1 \amp T_0 \amp = 1</mrow>
            <mrow>P_1 \amp = 1 \amp Q_1 \amp = 1 \amp T_1 \amp = 1</mrow>
            <mrow>P_2 \amp = 1 \amp Q_2 \amp = 2 \amp T_2 \amp = 1</mrow>
            <mrow>P_3 \amp = 1 \amp Q_3 \amp = 3 \amp T_3 \amp = 1</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          For the next stage:
          <md>
            <mrow>P_{0..2} \amp = P_0 P_1 = 1 \amp Q_{0..2} \amp = Q_0 Q_1 =  1 \amp
        T_{0..2} =  \amp T_0 Q_1 + P_0 T_1  = 2 </mrow>
            <mrow>P_{2..4} \amp = P_2 P_3 = 1 \amp Q_{2..4} \amp = Q_2 Q_3 = 6 \amp
        T_{2..4} =  \amp T_2 Q_3 + P_2 T_3  = 4 </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          and the last stage is
          <md>
            <mrow>P_{0..4} \amp = P_{0..2} P_{2..4} = 1  \amp
          Q_{0..4} = \amp Q_{0..2} Q_{2..4} = 6 </mrow>
            <mrow> T_{0..4} \amp = T_{0..2} Q_{2..4} + P_{0..2} T_{2..4} = 2(6) + (1)(4) = 16
        </mrow>
          </md>
          and the resulting fraction is
          <md>
            \frac{T_{0..4}}{Q_{0..4}} = \frac{16}{6} = \frac{8}{3}
          </md>
          and this is the same as the first four terms or
          <md>
            1 + \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{6} = \frac{16}{6} = \frac{8}{3}
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-binary-search-implementation">
    <title>Implementing Binary Splitting</title>

    <p>
      The previous section explains how binary splitting was used to rewrite a partial sum of an infinite series.
      We will now implement this.
    </p>

    <p>
      The key to this is to write the splitting in <xref ref="eq-splitting-1"/>.
      At each stage, we want to calculate the terms <m>P, Q</m> and <m>T</m>.
      However, at the lowest level (that is, the leaves of the tree), we need to use the arctan series for this.
      If we define
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
      arctan_terms(k) = k== 0 ? (P=1, Q=1, T=1) : (P=-1*(2k-1), Q= 2k+1, T=-1*(2k-1))
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      then this function returns the <m>P, Q, T</m> for each <m>k</m>.
      We can test this with the first few terms with:
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
        [arctan_terms(k) for k=0:3]
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      and the result is
    </p>

    <p>
      <cd>
        <cline>4-element Vector{@NamedTuple{P::Int64, Q::Int64, T::Int64}}:</cline>
        <cline>(P =  1, Q = 1, T =  1)</cline>
        <cline>(P = -1, Q = 3, T = -1)</cline>
        <cline>(P = -3, Q = 5, T = -3)</cline>
        <cline>(P = -5, Q = 7, T = -5)</cline>
      </cd>
    </p>

    <p>
      which is what we was saw in <xref ref="eq-splitting-example-leaves"/>.
      Next, we write the splitting term as a recursive formula:
    </p>

    <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
      <code>
          function binary_split(a::Integer, b::Integer, init::Function)
            if b - a == 1
              return init(a)
            else
              m = (a + b) ÷ 2
              left = binary_split(a, m, init)
              right = binary_split(m, b, init)
              return (P=left.P*right.P, Q=left.Q*right.Q, T=left.T * right.Q + left.P * right.T)
            end
          end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      This takes in the min and max of the terms and the initialization formula.
      The first branch of the if statement returns the series if there is only one term (when <m>b-a=1</m> ) otherwise,
      it splits the terms in half, and calls <c>binary_split</c> recursively on each halves.
      The return on line 8 is the formula in <xref ref="eq-splitting-1"/>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The initialization was tested above.
      The next level can be found with
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
          binary_split(0,2, arctan_terms), binary_split(2,4, arctan_terms)
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      and this returns
    </p>

    <p>
      <cd>
        <cline>((P = -1, Q = 3, T = 2), (P = 15, Q = 35, T = -6))</cline>
      </cd>
    </p>

    <p>
      which is the results from <xref ref="ex-splitting-ex-level-2"/>.
      The four-term partial sum can be found with
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
          four_terms = binary_split(0, 4, arctan_terms)
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      which returns
    </p>

    <p>
      <cd>
        <cline>(P = -15, Q = 105, T = 76)</cline>
      </cd>
    </p>

    <p>
      and again this was the result from above.
      The exact value of the partial sum can be written as <c>four_terms.T//four_terms.Q</c> and a floating point
      approximation is <c>four_terms.T/four_terms.Q</c>
    </p>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Reproduce the four terms of <m>e</m> that you found by hand in <xref ref="ex-bin-split-exp"/> using the code
          in this section.
          Note: you will need to write a <c>exp_terms</c> function that finds the values of the leaves.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          We start with the initialization function for the leaves or
        </p>

        <program language="julia">
          <code>
          exp_terms = arctan_terms(k) = k== 0 ? (P=1, Q=1, T=1) : (P=1, Q=k, T=1)
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The leaves are calculated with
        </p>

        <program language="julia">
          <code>
          [exp_terms(k) for k=0:3]
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          resulting in
        </p>

        <p>
          <cd>
            <cline>4-element Vector{@NamedTuple{P::Int64, Q::Int64, T::Int64}}:</cline>
            <cline>(P = 1, Q = 1, T = 1)</cline>
            <cline>(P = 1, Q = 1, T = 1)</cline>
            <cline>(P = 1, Q = 2, T = 1)</cline>
            <cline>(P = 1, Q = 3, T = 1)</cline>
          </cd>
        </p>

        <p>
          The next level of terms are found with
        </p>

        <program language="julia">
          <code>
          binary_split(0,2, exp_terms), binary_split(2,4, exp_terms)
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          resulting in
        </p>

        <p>
          <cd>
            <cline>((P = 1, Q = 1, T = 2), (P = 1, Q = 6, T = 4))</cline>
          </cd>
        </p>

        <p>
          and finally the sum of the first four terms is:
        </p>

        <program language="julia">
          <code>
          binary_split(0,4,exp_terms)
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          with the result
        </p>

        <p>
          <cd>
            <cline>(P = 1, Q = 6, T = 16)</cline>
          </cd>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </section>

  <section>
    <title>Extending the series to lots of terms</title>

    <p>
      The sections above were used to demonstrate how to perform binary splitting on a series.
      Each of the examples used 4 terms so numbers weren't overwhelming.
      However, we hope to use this for quite a few terms for practical use.
      We also return to the series for <m>e</m> so we can compare to results at the beginning of the section.
    </p>

    <p>
      In short, with the <c>binary_search</c> function that we wrote above, we just need to call that function with more
      terms.
      We will use a power of 2 since binary splitting is most efficient for that (see in the next section for why).
    </p>

    <p>
      For an example that is large but not too large, let try
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
          binary_split(0,64,exp_terms)
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      which returns <c>(P = 1, Q = 1585267068834414592, T = -2674445815446327044)</c>.
      The value of <m>P</m> is expected since all values of <m>P</m> are 1 and this is the product of them.
      However, the other two are odd.
      <m>Q</m> should be a factorial and after <m>5</m>, the factorial should end in a 0.
      And <m>T</m> is negative is strange.
      There are no negatives in any of the formulas, so where did those get introduced?
    </p>

    <p>
      The answer as we have seen before in this book is that there was Overflow.
      We mentioned that <m>Q</m> will be a factorial and that grows quickly and overflows around 50.
      So let's just shift to bigs and this is simply done with
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
          e64 = binary_split(big(0),big(64),exp_terms)
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      and this returns
    </p>

    <p>
      <cd>
        <cline>(P = 1, Q = 1982608315404440064116146708361898137544773690227268628106279599612729753600000000000000, </cline>
        <cline> T = 5389288156715688797574170724637602641365768984616696724053991331785579414134712244547836)</cline>
      </cd>
    </p>

    <p>
      which looks better.
      The value of <m>e</m> will be the ratio of <m>T/Q</m> and if we use floating point division with
      <c>e64.T/e64.Q</c>, the result is
    </p>

    <p>
      <cd>
        <cline>2.718281828459045235360287471352662497757247093699959574966967627724076630353555</cline>
      </cd>
    </p>

    <p>
      And we can use <c>num_digits_e(e64.T/e64.Q)</c> function to see how accuracy this is.
      The result is <c>77</c>, which you should notice is the limit of the default BigFloat.
      We didn't specify the precision of the BigFloat anywhere, so the default of 256 binary digits is used.
    </p>

    <note>
      <p>
        Although we are using a different technique to calculate the partial sum, the result for a given number of terms
        should be exactly the same that we did using a for loop above.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>
      Above, we were interesting in calculating this to about 1000 digits and noted that we needed precision of
      <m>2^{12}</m> for the BigFloats and needed about 500 terms.
      So if we use this precision with <m>2^9=512</m> terms then we should be comparable to above:
    </p>

    <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
      <code>
        setprecision(2^12) do
          e_approx = @btime let
            a = binary_split(big(0), big(128), exp_terms)
            a.T/a.Q
          end
          num_digits_e(e_approx)
        end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      results in <c>1166</c> digits of accuracy in <c>115.041 μs</c>, more than 50% faster than the fastest for-loop
      implementation above.
    </p>

    <note>
      <p>
        The code above might look strange because of the <c>let</c> block inside and perhaps the order of terms inside
        and around it.
        If you were not timing the calculation, I would not use a <c>let</c> block, but since you would like to know how
        much time the two lines (3 and 4) are using, we need to time those two and the easiest is as you see above.
        We put the <c>@btime</c> macro in front of the <c>let</c> because the <c>let</c> block is being timed.
        Then the <c>e_approx = </c> is the result of the <c>let</c> block.
      </p>
    </note>
  </section>

  <section>
    <title>Algorithmic Analysis of Binary Splitting</title>

    <p/>
  </section>
</chapter>